# plugin UIs — ALL Module Federation remotes, built from a CLEAN GIT CHECKOUT
# and served by ONE nginx.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY A CONTAINER AT ALL — UI PLUGINS ARE NORMALLY CDN ARTIFACTS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Upstream never containerises a UI plugin. Every `.github/workflows/ci-ui-*.yml`
# runs `pnpm nx build <plugin>_ui` on the runner and then
#   aws s3 sync dist/frontend/plugins/<plugin>_ui s3://erxes-next/$FOLDER/<plugin>_ui/
# i.e. the artifact is pushed to erxes' R2 bucket behind plugins.erxes.io. That
# is why no UI plugin in this repo has a Dockerfile of its own.
#
# We cannot use that CDN. `origin` here is github.com/hypedrive-app/erxes, a
# FORK; our WhatsApp + Plivo frontline_ui is not on plugins.erxes.io, and the
# builds that ARE there are upstream's, whose GraphQL documents would not match
# our *_api schemas. Publishing to our own R2 bucket would work but adds a
# second deploy channel (CI creds, bucket, custom domain). Since Dokploy already
# clones this repo and runs compose, serving the same `dist/` trees from a
# container in the same stack is strictly less moving parts.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE IMAGE FOR ALL PLUGINS — THIS IS THE UPSTREAM DISTRIBUTION MODEL
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file REPLACES the three per-plugin Dockerfile.build recipes that used to
# live in frontline_ui/, sales_ui/ and operation_ui/ (now under archive/).
#
# It is not a packaging convenience — it is what upstream actually does. All ten
# ci-ui-*.yml workflows sync into the SAME bucket under a per-plugin prefix:
#   s3://erxes-next/$FOLDER/frontline_ui/
#   s3://erxes-next/$FOLDER/sales_ui/
#   s3://erxes-next/$FOLDER/operation_ui/   … and seven more
# One bucket, one CDN host, N folders. There is no per-plugin host anywhere in
# the model, and core-api's remoteEntry() helper encodes exactly that: it takes
# ONE base URL and lets the PATH select the plugin (see the next section).
#
# The per-plugin-container variant appeared to work only because frontline was
# alone on the host. Adding sales and operation forced a choice between a
# hostname per plugin (three DNS names + three Dokploy Domains for three static
# file trees, and PLUGIN_CDN_URL is a SINGLE value so it cannot address them
# all) or Traefik path routing on one hostname. The latter was attempted and
# does not work: Dokploy's PathPrefix Domain attaches an `addprefix` middleware,
# which re-prepends the very prefix the request already carries
#   /latest/sales_ui/remoteEntry.js -> /latest/sales_ui/latest/sales_ui/remoteEntry.js
# -> 404, and `stripprefix` fails symmetrically by removing the prefix the file
# actually lives under. Both are workarounds for a self-inflicted problem.
# Serving all the prefixes from one docroot removes the problem instead: one
# hostname, one Dokploy Domain, no middleware, and PLUGIN_CDN_URL keeps being
# the single value core-api expects. Do not reintroduce path routing.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE URL LAYOUT IS LOAD-BEARING: /<version>/<plugin>_ui/remoteEntry.js
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# core-api answers GET /get-frontend-plugins with
#   `${PLUGIN_CDN_URL}/${version}/${plugin}_ui/remoteEntry.js`
# (backend/core-api/src/modules/organization/routes.ts). `version` comes from
# getPluginVersion() = `pluginInfo?.config?.releaseVersion || 'latest'`, so with
# no releaseVersion configured the browser requests
#   ${PLUGIN_CDN_URL}/latest/frontline_ui/remoteEntry.js
#   ${PLUGIN_CDN_URL}/latest/sales_ui/remoteEntry.js
#   ${PLUGIN_CDN_URL}/latest/operation_ui/remoteEntry.js
# from that ONE origin. The COPYs below therefore land each build at
#   /usr/share/nginx/html/latest/<plugin>_ui/
# which reproduces the exact `$FOLDER/<plugin>_ui/` shape the CI s3 sync
# creates. Keep the two in lockstep: this path IS the contract with routes.ts.
#
# `latest` is baked rather than parameterised on purpose. It is the value
# getPluginVersion() falls back to, and the erxes admin UI can set a per-plugin
# releaseVersion at runtime — but a container can only serve directories that
# exist at build time, so honouring an arbitrary runtime version would require
# fetching artifacts on boot. If you ever set a releaseVersion in the org
# config, add a matching directory here; otherwise leave it alone.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADDING A PLUGIN
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A name in ENABLED_PLUGINS makes core-api emit a remote entry URL for it, so
# the browser WILL request /latest/<plugin>_ui/remoteEntry.js from this origin
# and Module Federation init() rejects on a 404. Adding a plugin therefore means
# three edits HERE, not a new service: one `nx build`, one `test -f` assertion,
# and one COPY in the serve stage. Nothing in deploy/docker-compose.yml changes.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BUILD COST (measured, not estimated)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MEASURED per plugin via `/usr/bin/time -v pnpm nx build <p> --skip-nx-cache`
# on this tree: frontline_ui peak RSS 1.88 GB / ~48 s / 13 MB of output;
# sales_ui 1.69 GB / ~21 s / 7.5 MB; operation_ui 1.65 GB / ~14 s / 6.7 MB.
# The three `RUN`s are SEQUENTIAL, so peak memory is the max (~1.9 GB), not the
# sum — consolidating does not raise the OOM ceiling. What it does remove is two
# redundant `pnpm install`s: that step dominates first-deploy time, and it now
# happens once instead of three times.
#
# For contrast, the backend plugin builds are reported to peak near 13.9 GB
# against ~9.9 GB free on the Hostinger box (not re-measured here). The
# order-of-magnitude gap is what matters: an OOMed compose build wedges
# Dokploy's queue (dokploy#4461) with no working Cancel, taking the whole stack
# down, and these builds fit comfortably.
#
# NOTE ON TYPECHECK: `nx build <plugin>_ui` is an @nx/rspack:rspack target and
# does NOT gate on `tsc`. sales_ui currently has 4 known type errors confined to
# its POS module (IPos lacks a `type` field; a stale SalesFilter call site).
# They do not fail this build — verified, exit 0 — and POS is not deployed here.
# Do not "fix" them from this file; if a typecheck target is ever added to the
# build chain, this image will start failing and that is the correct signal.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BUILD CONTEXT IS THE REPO ROOT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   docker build -f frontend/plugins/Dockerfile.plugins .
# Nx, the pnpm workspace and tsconfig.base.json aliases only resolve from the
# root. It sits in frontend/plugins/ rather than in any one plugin directory
# because it belongs to none of them. Named `.plugins` rather than `.build`
# because it is not the per-project build recipe the `*_api/Dockerfile.build`
# convention names — there is no `frontend/plugins` project.

# ── Stage 1: compile every remote ────────────────────────────────────────────
FROM node:22-alpine3.22 AS build
WORKDIR /app

# Same Nx environment as the backend Dockerfile.build files — see
# backend/plugins/frontline_api/Dockerfile.build for why NX_SOCKET_DIR is NOT
# redundant with NX_DAEMON=false (nx asserts socket paths <= 95 chars and pnpm's
# virtual store blows past it on the plugin-worker path, surfacing as a
# cause-free "Failed to process project graph"). The ci-ui-*.yml workflows
# likewise export NX_DAEMON=false.
ENV NX_DAEMON=false \
    NX_SOCKET_DIR=/tmp/nx-sock \
    NX_REJECT_UNKNOWN_LOCAL_CACHE=0 \
    CI=true

RUN npm install -g pnpm@9

# Whole tree, not a curated subset: nx.json's inference plugins load
# eslint.config.js / jest.config.ts / jest.preset.js from the ROOT while
# crawling the project graph. .dockerignore already strips node_modules, dist,
# .git and .nx. Same trap documented at length in frontline_api/Dockerfile.build.
COPY . .

# ONCE for all three plugins — they share this workspace. This is the dominant
# cost of the image; the compiles below are comparatively cheap.
RUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile

# rspack + Module Federation -> dist/frontend/plugins/<plugin>_ui/
#
# One layer, chained with && rather than three RUNs: these targets share the nx
# project graph and the same node_modules, and splitting them buys no useful
# cache granularity (any source change invalidates the preceding `COPY . .`
# anyway) while paying for three graph crawls.
#
# operation_ui's target additionally emits src/assets (declared in its
# project.json `assets`), which lands beside remoteEntry.js and is served by the
# `location /` block in nginx/default.conf.
RUN pnpm nx build frontline_ui \
 && pnpm nx build sales_ui \
 && pnpm nx build operation_ui

# Fail HERE rather than shipping an nginx that 404s the one file that matters.
# Per plugin, because a partial build is the failure mode this catches: MF
# init() rejects on the FIRST missing remote, so one absent remoteEntry.js takes
# down the whole UI, not just its own plugin's screens.
RUN test -f dist/frontend/plugins/frontline_ui/remoteEntry.js \
 && test -f dist/frontend/plugins/sales_ui/remoteEntry.js \
 && test -f dist/frontend/plugins/operation_ui/remoteEntry.js \
 && echo "frontline_ui + sales_ui + operation_ui build OK"

# ── Stage 2: static server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
FROM nginx:alpine

# See the URL-layout note above: these directory shapes are the contract with
# core-api's remoteEntry() helper, not a stylistic choice. Serving all three
# from one docroot is what lets a single PLUGIN_CDN_URL address every plugin.
COPY --from=build /app/dist/frontend/plugins/frontline_ui \
                  /usr/share/nginx/html/latest/frontline_ui
COPY --from=build /app/dist/frontend/plugins/sales_ui \
                  /usr/share/nginx/html/latest/sales_ui
COPY --from=build /app/dist/frontend/plugins/operation_ui \
                  /usr/share/nginx/html/latest/operation_ui

# The frontline_ui vhost, reused verbatim for all three. Its rules are per-URL,
# not per-plugin: no-cache on the stable-named remoteEntry.js manifests,
# immutable on the content-hashed chunks, CORS on fonts. Nothing in it names a
# plugin, and the three per-plugin copies it was forked into differed only in
# comments. It lives under frontline_ui/ because that is where it was written;
# it is the shared config now, so edit it there.
COPY frontend/plugins/frontline_ui/nginx/default.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf

EXPOSE 80
